<h2>The challenge</h2><p>Natural-language flexibility creates ambiguity. A system must resolve user intent, choose relevant data, handle unsupported questions, and communicate uncertainty. A fluent response is not itself proof that the numbers are correct.</p>
<h2>Technical approach</h2><ul><li>Define the allowed analytical questions, metric vocabulary, and data boundaries.</li><li>Design the flow between a user query, data-access tools, and a structured response.</li><li>Apply validation and guardrails to avoid presenting unsupported conclusions as facts.</li><li>Evaluate the end-to-end experience for correctness, clarity, and recoverability.</li></ul>
<h2>The judgment behind the work</h2><p>Successful AI engineering requires balancing flexible interactions against controlled data access and consistent business semantics. The architecture should make the source of an answer traceable.</p>
<div class="callout">Why this project is here: it illustrates a combination of modeling judgment, engineering tradeoffs, and attention to the decisions the system supports—not a claim of performance without evidence.</div>
<h2>Evidence to add</h2><p>A future public demo should use synthetic data and explicitly cover correctness checks, unsupported requests, and system limitations.</p>
